# NASF — example crontab for the scheduled jobs described in PLAN.md
# Phase 7. Install with `crontab -e` as the deploy user (not root), after
# adjusting the path and virtualenv activation to match the target host.
#
# Every command below is safe to run more than once for the same period —
# each wraps itself in a ScheduledJobRun keyed by (name, period_key) and
# no-ops if that period's run already succeeded. That's what makes a
# duplicate cron fire (e.g. after a host reboot) harmless, and it's also
# how apps.core.views.audit / the Backups admin page detects a *dead*
# cron: query the latest ScheduledJobRun per `name` and alert if it's
# older than its expected interval.
#
# Adjust REPO_PATH and the venv path for your deployment.

REPO_PATH=/srv/nasf/backend
VENV=/srv/nasf/backend/venv

# Document expiry alerts — daily at 06:00
0 6 * * * cd $REPO_PATH && $VENV/bin/python manage.py check_document_expiry >> /var/log/nasf/check_document_expiry.log 2>&1

# Vacation accrual — first of the month at 00:15 (idempotent per calendar month,
# so a delayed/duplicate run on the 1st is harmless)
15 0 1 * * cd $REPO_PATH && $VENV/bin/python manage.py accrue_vacation >> /var/log/nasf/accrue_vacation.log 2>&1

# Encrypted DB backup — daily at 02:00. BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_KEY and
# BACKUP_ROOT must be set in the environment (see .env.example) — BACKUP_ROOT
# should be a path OUTSIDE this host's normal backup rotation of the DB
# disk itself, per PLAN.md's "off the DB disk" requirement.
0 2 * * * cd $REPO_PATH && $VENV/bin/python manage.py backup_db >> /var/log/nasf/backup_db.log 2>&1

# restore_db is intentionally NOT scheduled here — it is destructive
# (overwrites the live database) and must only ever be run manually:
#   python manage.py restore_db --yes [--backup-id N]
# Test it periodically as a restore drill, against a throwaway database,
# not production.
